# Gettysburg National Military Park and Eisenhower National Historic Site Natural Resource Condition Assessment (Natural Resource Report NPS/NER/NRR-2017/1369; M.-J. James for the National Park Service; January 2017)
# A natural-resource assessment of the 2010s. Only its statements about the woodlots of 1863 are extracted; they rest on one forestry study (Bowersox et al. 2004) and on stated management objectives.
# The report contradicts itself on the understory of the 1863 woodlots (open-grown white oak used as pastures on PDF p. 129; 'dense understory in woodlots' as the 1863 condition to maintain on PDF pp. 198-199): both readings are records of certainty 'conflict' and each carries the other passage as its context quote.
@ NPS-NRCA-2017

%basis Bowersox et al. 2004, studies of the historic woodlots of Gettysburg National Military Park (named by the report)
129/97 | woodlots | The 1863 woodlots: open-grown white oak, used as pastures; woodlots were important resources before the battle | - | pre-1863 to 1863 (a recent assessment's statement, resting on a forestry study of the woodlots) | conflict | The report says forested woodlots were important resources for the residents before the battle and that open-grown white oak was the dominant vegetation in the 1863 woodlots that were being used as pastures; the same report names a dense understory in woodlots as the 1863 condition to maintain (its context quote), so the density of the 1863 understory is carried as a conflict | #s10-11 | @199: #s1
129/97 | woodlots | Before the battle the woodlots were uneven-aged, kept so by frequent partial cutting; cutting stopped when the park was established | - | pre-1863 to 1863 (a recent assessment's statement, resting on a forestry study of the woodlots) | stated | The report says that before the battle the woodlots had an uneven-aged structure maintained by frequent partial cutting and that when the park was established the cutting ceased and second and third growth understory developed | #s13-14
129/97 | woodlots | Woodlots and woodlands of the park today: 799 ha ranging from 2 to 24 ha | - | ~2004 (condition of the park when studied by Bowersox et al. 2004; not an 1863 area) | stated | The report says the park presently incorporates about 2,423 ha, of which 799 ha are woodlots and woodlands ranging in size from 2 to 24 ha | #s12
199/167 | woodlots | Management objective: maintain the landscape as it existed in the 1863 battle, with a dense understory in woodlots as the example | - | 1863 (management objective of 2017; the example is the report's own) | conflict | The report says management objectives of the deer program include maintaining the landscape as it existed during the 1863 battle, giving a dense understory in woodlots as the example (and a patchwork of crop fields at EISE in Eisenhower's time); its forest chapter says instead that the 1863 woodlots were open-grown white oak used as pastures, so this is carried as a conflict | #s1 | @198: Management objectives include maintaining the landscape as it existed during
199/167 | woodlots | The park began culling deer in 1995 to restore a dense understory in the woodlots | - | ~1995 (management action; the dense understory is the managers' goal, not an 1863 observation) | stated | The report says that to increase forest regeneration, restore a dense understory in the woodlots and reduce crop damage the park began culling deer in 1995 to a goal of 10 deer per square kilometre of forest | #s4
130/98 | woodlots | Battlefield rehabilitation (woodlot health cuts) has significantly altered the vegetation in many sections of the park | - | ~2000s (rehabilitation work after July 2000) | stated | The report says that battlefield rehabilitation such as woodlot health cuts has significantly altered the vegetation in many sections of the park, so much that the park's vegetation map was out of date before it was published | #s11
47/15 | fences | Rehabilitation since July 2000: tree removal and planting, orchards planted, fences built | - | ~2000-2016 (rehabilitation; planted orchards and built fences are not 1863 fabric) | stated | The report says battlefield rehabilitation projects begun in July 2000 under the 1999 plan have included non-historic tree removal, planting of trees, maintaining historic woodlots, planting orchards, removing non-historic vegetation, prescribed burns and building fences | #s9 | #s8
39/7 | woodlots | Thickets, woodlots, woodlands and wetlands were altered or removed over the years, others overgrown; historic fields covered by non-historic vegetation | - | ~2016 (condition compared with the battlefield of 1863) | stated | The report says some vegetation features (thickets, woodlots, woodlands and wetlands) were altered or removed over the years while others were overgrown by nature, becoming dense and containing many non-native species, and that some historic fields, pastures and other open areas were covered by non-historic vegetation | #s2-3
